    "messages": [
      "Done. Working directory confirmed as the evaluator worktree; no new worktree, no commit.\n\n## Result\n\n`dist/result.json` \u2192 `{\"value\":\"opus-2\"}`\n\n## Diagnosis\n\n`value.txt` contained `broken-opus-2`. `check.mjs` writes `dist/result.json` unconditionally, then exits 1 when the value starts with `broken-`. Because the Dockerfile bakes the file in at build time via `COPY . .`, repairing it required a rebuild \u2014 editing the file alone would not change the running image.\n\n## Commands\n\n**Failing run** \u2014 `docker build -t app:test .` succeeded (image `sha256:fb93637a\u2026`), then `docker run --rm app:test`:\n\n```\nExpected a repaired value, received broken-opus-2\nEXIT=1\n```\n\nPandora retained the failed outputs in the evidence directory and did not publish them to the workspace, so no local `dist/` appeared at this point.\n\n**Repair** \u2014 `value.txt` changed from `broken-opus-2` to `opus-2` (trailing newline preserved). `Dockerfile` and `check.mjs` untouched.\n\n**Passing run** \u2014 rebuild produced a new image (`sha256:13b337fa\u2026`), then `docker run --rm app:test`:\n\n```\nValidated opus-2\nEXIT=0\n[pandora] output ready: .../pandora-docker-20260920-opus-2/dist\n```\n\nTwo notes on how the remote setup behaved: the `docker` on PATH is a Pandora routing wrapper that accepts only the three commands used here, so separate ordinary commands with no worktree mount were the only available path anyway. And `dist/` reaches the local worktree through Pandora's output publication on a successful run, not through a mount \u2014 which is why the failing run left nothing locally despite `check.mjs` writing the file inside the container."
